{"verdict": "revise",
  "hypothesis_status": {"H1": "refuted", "H2": "supported", "H3": "supported"},
  "issues": [
    "C1/E1 misread ground-truth: NWM has NO DATA for Oct 22 CDT (02:00 UTC Oct 23) per coverage window (NWM starts 10/23 2 AM CDT = 07:00 UTC Oct 23). The finding claims NWM 'missed' the pulse and 'peaked one hour later (5,516 cfs at 22:00 CDT)' — but ground-truth shows NWM data begins Oct 23 CDT, and on Oct 23 CDT NWM peaked at 5,399 cfs at 20:00 CDT (01:00 UTC Oct 24), lagging USGS peak (19:00 CDT) by +1 h. The 5,516 cfs figure and Oct 22 CDT NWM peak are fabricated; E1 rows correctly show NWM NULL at 02:00–06:00 UTC Oct 23. H1 must be refuted: NWM absence is a data-coverage gap, not an assimilation-cycle miss.",
    "C2/E2 correctly show USGS and NWS Oliver tailwater stages match (≤0.04 ft), confirming same sensor. However, the flow comparison mixes gauges: NWS Oliver cfs vs USGS Northport cfs are at different hydraulic control points (Oliver tailwater vs Northport pool). The Oct 19 02:00 UTC example (NWS 1,497.5 cfs vs USGS Northport 5,870 cfs) compares Oliver outflow to Northport pool inflow during a hydropeaking pulse — they differ because the pool is filling, not because of rating-curve differences. The systematic ~300–400 cfs higher NWS daily mean (C4) is the valid evidence for H2.",
    "C3/E3 rows are malformed: they show multiple rows per day with different peak hours (e.g., Oct 18 has peaks at hours 21, 20, 22, 23, 3, 2), not a single daily maximum. Ground-truth confirms the daily 15-min peak occurs at 21:00 CDT on Oct 18–22. The query must return ONE row per day (the hour of the daily max peak). H3 is supported by ground-truth, but E3 does not prove it.",
    "C4/E4 correctly supports H2: daily mean USGS Northport (675–938 cfs) vs NWS Oliver (987–1,188 cfs) differ by 279–448 cfs while pool-stage changes are ~1.1 ft/day — negligible storage effect. This confirms the flow gap is a rating-curve/hydraulic-control difference, not storage.",
    "C1 and C3 contradict ground-truth on NWM coverage and peak timing. C2's flow comparison conflates two different hydraulic points. Only C4 cleanly supports its hypothesis."
  ],
  "new_checks": [
    {"id": "C5", "hypothesis": "H3", "purpose": "Extract the single CDT hour of the daily maximum 15-min peak (usgs_northport_peak_cfs) for each full local day Oct 18–24 to confirm the 21:00 CDT recurrence.", "how": "SELECT local_day, peak_hour_cdt, max_peak_cfs FROM (SELECT to_date(from_utc_timestamp(to_timestamp(hr_utc, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm'), 'America/Chicago')) AS local_day, hour(from_utc_timestamp(to_timestamp(hr_utc, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm'), 'America/Chicago')) AS peak_hour_cdt, usgs_northport_peak_cfs AS max_peak_cfs, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY to_date(from_utc_timestamp(to_timestamp(hr_utc, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm'), 'America/Chicago')) ORDER BY usgs_northport_peak_cfs DESC) AS rn FROM water.hourly_compare WHERE hr_utc >= '2026-10-18 06:00' AND hr_utc < '2026-10-25 06:00' AND usgs_northport_peak_cfs IS NOT NULL) WHERE rn = 1 ORDER BY local_day"},
    {"id": "C6", "hypothesis": "H1", "purpose": "Show NWM values on the two days it has data (Oct 23–24 CDT) at the USGS peak hour (19:00 CDT = 00:00 UTC) and the following hour to verify the +1 h lag and magnitude match ground-truth (5,399 cfs at 20:00 CDT on Oct 23; 5,108 cfs at 20:00 CDT on Oct 24).", "how": "SELECT hr_utc, hour(from_utc_timestamp(to_timestamp(hr_utc, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm'), 'America/Chicago')) AS hour_cdt, usgs_northport_cfs, usgs_northport_peak_cfs, nwm_cfs FROM water.hourly_compare WHERE hr_utc IN ('2026-10-24 00:00','2026-10-24 01:00','2026-10-25 00:00','2026-10-25 01:00') AND nwm_cfs IS NOT NULL ORDER BY hr_utc"}
  ]}
